states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }{\\color{blue}\\text{invertible}}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is \"invertible\"."] [17592186272335 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }{\\color{blue}\\text{invertible}}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=n=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is \"invertible\"."] [17592186272337 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}\\text{Col}(A)} &=\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{Col}(A).$"] [17592186312695 :answer/value "\\mathbf{b}\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}"] [17592186313716 :answer/value "\\mathbf{b}\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}"] [17592186316342 :answer/value "\\mathbf{b}\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}"] [17592186317792 :answer/value "\\mathbf{b}\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}"] [17592186339313 :answer/value "\\mathbf{b}\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}"] [17592186416934 :answer/value "\\mathbf{x}\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\}"] [17592186422770 :answer/value "\\mathbf{b}\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}"] [17592186422778 :answer/value "\\mathbf{b}\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}"] [17592186426060 :answer/value "\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}"] [17592186426062 :answer/value "{\\mathbb{R}}^{n}"] [17592186426850 :answer/value "\\mathbf{x}\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\}"] [17592186430722 :answer/value "\\mathbf{x}\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2},{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\\}"] [17592186455244 :answer/value "\\mathbf{x}\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\}"] [17592186630980 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(E),$ we need to check if the equation $Ex=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[E\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[E\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -4 & 1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -7 & -2 & -1 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -4 & 1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -7 & -2 & -1 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -4 & 1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & -2 & 11\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -4 & 1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 10\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} &=-6 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4} &=-1 \\\\ 0 &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the  third equation, we get a false statement $0=10.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(E).$"] [17592186630988 :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(H),$ then the equation $Hx=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[H\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[H\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & k+1 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & k+1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & k+1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3k-1 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & k+1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3k-1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6k+7\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & k+1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3k-1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3k+6\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=k+1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-3k-1 \\\\ 0 &=3k+6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=3k+6,$ which means that $k=-2.$"] [17592186670037 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(F),$ we need to check if the equation $Fx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[F\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[F\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{2} &=-4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=4.$ Substituting into the first equation gives  ${x}_{1}=5.$  \n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Fx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(F).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${f}_{1}$ and ${f}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=5\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{f}_{1}}+4\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}}_{{f}_{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=5\\cdot 4=20.$"]}
